WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.702
A major development in the banking world.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.471
The FDIC just reported the California

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:07.040
regulators shut down Silicon Valley Bank,

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.242
a big lender out in California,

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:09.909
after reporting

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:11.811
a loss of over $1 billion.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.713
There were fears of a run on this bank.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:14.280
People wondering

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.549
if there should have PTSD to 2008.

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:17.384
What's going on here?

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:17.784
Yeah,

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.552
this really is an earthquake,

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.254
especially for the tech community.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:22.856
Silicon Valley Bank

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:25.759
dropped by 60% yesterday alone.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.660
We just learned today

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.263
that California regulators have shut this

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:31.331
lender down.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:34.434
This is the biggest failure since 2008.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.236
It's actually the second biggest failure

00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:38.304
ever since Washington Mutual

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.874
in September. Of 2008.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:43.443
This is a major lender tech startups.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.012
The FDIC says that insured depositors

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.547
they will have full access

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.416
to insured deposits

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:51.217
by Monday morning insured deposits

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.620
that means up to $250,000.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:54.220
Of course

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.790
we know that some small businesses,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.391
some startups, some individuals

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.693
they have more than that $250,000.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:01.795
It's not clear

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:02.362
if they're going

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.930
to get all of their money back.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.098
This, of course, is not sitting

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.833
well in the marketplace.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.001
We see the Dow is down

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.903
by more than 250 points.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.639
Banks are leading the way lower.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:13.306
The good news here

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:15.141
is that experts that I'm talking to,

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.010
they are hopeful that

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.112
this is an isolated incident,

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.180
that this is not part

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.315
of a broader systemic issue.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.150
Mark Zandi, he told me

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:24.951
he's the chief economist

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:25.885
over at Moody's Analytics.

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:26.686
He just told me that

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:27.854
he doesn't think

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:28.555
that this failure

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.790
is symptomatic of a broader problem

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.292
in the banking industry.

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:32.926
Let's hope not,

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.327
because that's the last thing

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:35.095
we need right now.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:35.428
You know,

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.262
the major banks are saying

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.064
that they are well-capitalized.

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:39.099
Matt Egan, thank you.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.102
Robert Reich, former U.S.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:43.703
labor secretary under President

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:44.471
Clinton, is with us

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.673
now, also the author of The System

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.109
Who Rigged It and How We Fix It.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.610
Secretary Rice, always good to have you.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.679
Let's start where Matt ended with this.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:54.781
Silicon Valley Bank,

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.016
the FDIC now in control.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.284
We saw that

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.286
there was some stumbles from Wells Fargo,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.489
JPMorgan Chase, Bank of America.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.456
Other banks

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:06.092
trading their halted yesterday.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.327
What does this mean

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.061
for people

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:11.164
outside of the Silicon Valley bank

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:12.966
universe?

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:13.399
What does it mean

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:16.503
for the average borrower, if anything?

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.338
So far, it doesn't mean anything.

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:20.840
But the big question is one of contagion.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.676
That is what we saw in 2008

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.612
was when one big bank

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:28.148
and a couple of others started to fail.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.582
Or could not pay

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:30.850
their depositors,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.286
could not actually pay up what they owed.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.221
They were closed

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.624
and regulators had to move in very,

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:39.025
very quickly.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:41.895
But we ended up with a financial crisis

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:45.031
because, you know, one big banking card

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.834
easily starts tipping over other cards.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.068
It's a house of cards.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:52.038
Now, we don't know yet about contagion.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.673
What we do know

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:54.040
is that

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.342
this bank was obviously overextended

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.545
and this is related to the Fed

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.780
because as interest rates went up

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.615
and as and this bank was lending

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.317
to a lot of startups,

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.619
this bank simply could not handle it.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:07.620
I think that

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.923
this is the biggest economic news today.

00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:11.324
It is not.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.960
This jobs report report was good.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.229
And it's kind of signals to me

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:17.297
a soft landing.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.598
But I think that in terms

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:20.667
of what the Fed is going to do

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.703
and this bank implosion

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:27.140
was potential contagion, may reverse.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.076
Jerome Powell has a direction

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.679
it may lead to instead of a half

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.081
a point increase at the March meeting.

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:37.250
It may lead to no increase.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.219
In fact, it's even conceivable

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.286
that interest rates

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:41.354
start dropping

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:42.722
out, but fear

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.924
that we're going to be in deep trouble.

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.926
You have advocated for the Fed

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.596
to stop increasing the interest rate.

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:52.298
Why before this issue with

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:53.433
Silicon Valley Bank

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:54.567
now?

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.503
Simply because I don't see any wage price

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.405
inflation, wages,

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.340
according to Jerome

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.975
Powell, are pushing up prices.

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.543
Well, that's simply not the case.

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:05.311
Look at today's report, for example,

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.312
we we are seeing

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:07.914
the smallest wage

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:09.782
increase in over a year.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.818
Prices continue to rise.

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.119
That is absolutely true.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.121
But wages are not pushing them up.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:15.421
It's not

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.256
that workers are doing

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:17.624
so wonderfully well.

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:18.391
And what's pushing up

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.125
many prices

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.427
domestically in big companies

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:22.862
that want to increase

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:23.930
their profit margins.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.132
And so they have monopolies

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.067
or near monopolies, oligopolies.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.902
They are using the opportunity,

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.038
using inflation as an excuse

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.473
to put up their prices.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:36.376
So this is at its at its bottom here.

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.311
It really is an anti-trust

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:39.646
monopolization issue.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:42.448
It is not a Fed interest rate problem.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.984
So you suggest that the Fed

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:45.418
should stop

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:46.886
increasing the interest rate,

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.022
but if that is the only tool they have.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:49.822
I imagine

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.024
you're going to correct that assumption.

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:52.292
But if that is the only tool

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.160
they have to try to bring it back

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.898
down, to the 2% rate, that goal, then

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.934
what else can they do to to to

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.736
try to tame inflation,

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.838
if not increase the interest rate?

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:07.340
Well, first of all,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:07.940
let me just say,

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.242
it's not clear

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.144
that they have to do anything else.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.546
Inflation is starting to come down.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.648
Now, we'll find out more Tuesday

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:17.050
when the inflation

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.251
report the consumer

00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:20.253
price in Greece, increased report,

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.188
inflation report will be coming out.

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:24.557
But as of what we know now,

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:27.193
inflation is slowing slightly.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.396
As the key here is the direction.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:31.230
You know,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.665
if inflation were increasing,

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:33.566
that would be one thing

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.601
but if inflation is starting to

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:36.502
slow down,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:37.270
if we're

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.272
seeing it going in the right direction,

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:40.306
then it's not clear

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:41.107
that the Fed has got

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:42.608
to keep raising interest rates

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.944
and risking a recession

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:46.379
that is going to hurt everybody.

